;; Clojure namespaces a `.cljc` module can keep requiring after it moves to
;; Kotoba, and -- as loudly as what is provided -- what is deliberately NOT
;; here.
;;
;; A shim is only worth having if it is exact. Kotoba's string surface is
;; addressed in UTF-8 bytes (`string-length` of "aあb" is 5; `string-code-point-at`
;; takes a byte offset; a split index REFUSES rather than returning something
;; wrong -- all measured 2026-08-31). Three of `clojure.string`'s functions are
;; indifferent to that. Five more (2026-09-02) are questions about code
;; points, and Kotoba controls its own encoding: the byte width of a code
;; point is a function of the code point, so a scan can walk a string code
;; point by code point and answer exactly what the JVM answers. The rest are
;; not, and providing them approximately under their real names would answer,
;; quietly, wrong.
;;
;; Where such a function is worth having anyway, it lives here under a
;; DIFFERENT name in a `kotoba.*` namespace, saying in the name what it
;; actually answers -- `kotoba.string/byte-index-of` for the byte-offset half
;; of `clojure.string/index-of`, `kotoba.string.case/simple-lower-case` for
;; the locale-free half of `clojure.string/lower-case`. The absent entry then points at it, so the
;; reason and the replacement are one lookup apart. A caller reaching for the
;; Clojure name has to stop and change the call, which is the moment to notice
;; that the number means something else now.
;;
;; Consumed through the ordinary project route, which needs no JVM:
;;   amu check app.cljk --source-path <app> --source-path <this repo>/lang/compat --jvm-free
{:kotoba.lang.compat/version 2
 ;; Version 2 (2026-09-02): the case mapping. kotoba.string.case answers
 ;; the Unicode SIMPLE mappings and SpecialCasing's unconditional
 ;; expansions, from generated tables; Character.toLowerCase(int) is the
 ;; oracle where the host JDK's Unicode version is current, and is behind
 ;; on Unicode 16.0 for U+019B / U+0264 / U+1C89 / U+1C8A on Temurin 21.
 ;; The three clojure.string case names stay absent, and their :reason is
 ;; no longer "unstarted" but the two things a table cannot fix -- the
 ;; ambient locale, and Final_Sigma.
 :kotoba.lang.compat/version-1 "the three byte-indifferent functions (starts-with? / ends-with? / includes?), the five code-point ones (blank? / trim / triml / trimr / reverse), and kotoba.string's index functions"
 :kotoba.lang.compat/authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
 :kotoba.lang.compat/workspace-adr
 "90-docs/adr/2608311750-cljk-migration-cost-is-not-effects.edn"

 :measured
 {:as-of "2026-09-02"
  :case-mapping "The Unicode SIMPLE mappings (UnicodeData.txt fields 12/13, 1:1 and locale-free) are what kotoba.string.case answers, from generated tables, swept over every code point below U+3100 plus every one the JVM maps plus the Unicode 16.0 rows a Unicode-15 JDK lacks. Character.toLowerCase(int) / toUpperCase(int) implement those mappings for the Unicode version the host JDK carries -- they are the oracle where that version is current, and they are behind on Unicode 16.0 for U+019B, U+0264, U+1C89, U+1C8A on Temurin 21 / Unicode 15.0. String.toLowerCase()/toUpperCase() are neither simple nor locale-free: they take Locale.getDefault() -- (.toLowerCase \"I\" tr) is \"\u0131\" -- and they are not 1:1 -- 102 code points expand under toUpperCase(ROOT) and one (U+0130) under toLowerCase(ROOT), plus the Final_Sigma condition, which depends on the surrounding string. That is why the three clojure.string case names stay absent while three kotoba.string.case names are provided."
  :string-length-is :utf8-bytes
  :evidence "(string-length \"aあb\") = 5, (string-byte-length \"aあb\") = 5, (string-code-point-at \"aあb\" 1) = 12354, (string-substring \"aあb\" 1 2) refused with \"string substring index splits a UTF-8 code point\" (2026-08-31); (string-code-point-at \"ab\" 2) traps \"string code-point offset is out of bounds\", so every scan here stops at the byte length before reading (2026-09-02)"
  :oracle "clojure.string itself, called from test/kotoba/lang/clojure_string_compat_test.cljk on the same inputs: 48 of 48 for the three 2026-08-31 functions (16 inputs x 3); the 2026-09-02 functions are compared on 26 whitespace-bearing inputs x 4 (blank?/trim/triml/trimr) + 14 inputs for reverse, and the whitespace predicate itself against java.lang.Character/isWhitespace for every code point below U+3100 plus every code point the JVM says is whitespace -- the counts the test prints are the record, this sentence is the shape"
  :regex-literal "a #\"...\" literal in guest source is a READER rejection (:kotoba/source-read-failed, \"source reader rejected input\"), not a value -- measured 2026-09-02, amu bb51dc14. So a string-only split/replace could not silently accept a pattern; that arity is refused before any function is called. It is not the reason those stay absent (see :absent split)."
  :list-of-string "CLOSED 2026-09-08, and the 2026-09-02 sentence was already half wrong when it was written. vector-at / vector-conj DO still refuse a [:list :string] (\"expression type mismatch: expected vector-i64, got [:list :string]\", exit 65, re-measured at amu 9092ee34 on 2026-09-08) -- and they always will: they are the bounded-vector operations and a list is a different carrier. The accessor is typed-list-nth, which kotoba-sema has carried since 2026-09-03 (sema ADR 0034, arriving with keys/vals whose projection IS a [:list T]); nth on a list desugars to it. What was actually missing on 2026-09-02 was not the accessor but its LOWERING: at amu 9092ee34, amu check returned exit 0 on (typed-list-nth [:list :string] v 1) while amu compile --target web returned exit 70 \"unsupported KIR node\" and --target wasm32 exit 70 \"typed Wasm operation is not qualified\" -- for the WHOLE module, whether the function was reached or not (two-function reproduction: main returns 7 and never calls the other, and both targets still refuse). Both are implemented now: kotoba-script emits typedListNth, kotoba-wasm emits list-nth-i64 / list-nth-ref against two conditionally imported intrinsics, and amu runtime/browser-host.mjs answers them. Executed, not only lowered: (nth (typed-list-new [:list :string] \"a\" \"bb\" \"ccc\") 1) is \"bb\" on BOTH backends, an out-of-range index traps on both, and a [:list :i64] reads back 30. A list of strings can now be built, counted and read."
  :list-construction "A [:list T] can only be CONSTRUCTED with a statically known item count. kotoba-sema's canonical-list-operations is exactly #{typed-list-new typed-list-nth} -- a constructor over literal items and an accessor, with nothing that grows one. Measured 2026-09-08 at amu 9092ee34, every candidate refused at check time: (conj l \"x\") gives \"conj requires a typed set [:set item-type]; got [:list :string]\"; (vector-conj l \"x\") gives \"expression type mismatch: expected vector-i64, got [:list :string]\"; typed-list-conj and concat give \"unknown operation ... is not a builtin, a sugar head, or a function of this module\"; (cons \"x\" l) types as the i64 pair chain instead. A vector literal in a [:list T] result position desugars to typed-list-new, so it is the same constructor. The only variable-length source of a list is typed-map-keys / typed-map-vals -- and those lower on the web backend only: (vector-count (typed-map-keys ...)) is exit 70 \"unsupported typed Wasm expression\" on wasm32, measured the same day. This is what now keeps split absent (see :absent split); it is NOT what kept join absent, and join is provided."
  :option-return-on-wasm32 "CLOSED 2026-09-08, amu 9092ee34. It was true on 2026-09-02 against amu bb51dc14: a function whose result is an option with the two constructors on the two arms of an if compiled on KIR and refused wasm32 AND wasm32-browser (\"unsupported typed Wasm expression\", exit 70), and refused the whole project even when main never reached it. Re-measured at amu 9092ee34 the same shape lowers, and the ARTIFACT RUNS: a two-module project (an option-returning fn called on both arms, plus a second option-returning fn never called from main) compiles to wasm32, wasm32-browser and web and answers 907 on all three through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs -- 7 from the some arm and 900 from the else of the none arm, so neither arm is collapsed. Guarded from here on by amu's conformance pilot case :option-return-kit, which RUNS on both the KIR and wasm32 backends and carries an uncalled option-returning fn on purpose. The second-order damage outlived the defect and is what this note is for: because ONE unreachable function could refuse a whole project, this compat surface was written to avoid option returns entirely, and an implementation state became a house style. index-of and last-index-of are the first two names moved back."}

 :modules
 {;; A DECLARED-TEMPORARY compat surface, not the home of anything.
  ;;
  ;; Until 2026-09-08 this held the definitions. The owner made `kotoba.string`
  ;; the canonical guest string library that day and they moved there; this
  ;; forwards, one line per name, so there is one implementation and no second
  ;; copy to drift.
  :clojure.string
  {:path "lang/compat/clojure/string.kotoba"
   :kind :shim
   :forwards-to kotoba.string
   :provides #{starts-with? ends-with? includes?
               blank? trim triml trimr reverse
               index-of last-index-of}
   :equivalence :exact
   :shim
   {:as-of "2026-09-08"
    :why-not-deleted "74 guest .kotoba modules require it, measured rather than assumed: `grep -rl --include='*.kotoba' '\\[clojure\\.string' orgs | wc -l` -> 74, of which network-awai/app-hyakka 66, kotoba-lang/loop-system-dynamics 6, kotoba-lang/lang-pure-wt 1, kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang 1. Deleting the namespace stops all 74 resolving their (:require [clojure.string :as str]). Rewriting those call sites is a separate migration with its own decision; a rename does not travel on the back of an unrelated change."
    :removal-condition "this file goes when that count reaches 0. Re-running the one command above is the whole of the check; nothing else has to be decided."
    :shape "forwarding wrappers, because a bare re-export is NOT expressible here. Measured 2026-09-08 at amu 9092ee34: (ns kotoba.text (:require [kotoba.string :as ks]) (:export [byte-index-of])) with no definition is refused :kotoba.error/export-names-undefined, \"namespace exports must name declared public functions: byte-index-of is not defined in this module\". So each name is a one-line call into kotoba.string -- one implementation, one extra call frame."
    :drift-guard "a forwarder that reimplemented its body, or forwarded to a different name, would compile and would pass every behaviour test, because those run the canonical module. test/kotoba/lang/clojure_string_compat_test.cljk reads the forwarders and asserts each is a single call to the SAME name in kotoba.string."
    :does-not-carry-join "join is in kotoba.string.join, not here. Putting it here would make all 74 call sites above need an amu that lowers typed-list-nth, which is the defect this arrangement exists to avoid -- measured, see :modules :kotoba.string.join :measured."}
   :absent
   {;; unchanged by the move: these are absent from the CANONICAL module for
    ;; the reasons recorded there, and a shim cannot forward what does not
    ;; exist. See :modules :kotoba.string :absent for each reason.
    lower-case {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent lower-case."}
    upper-case {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent upper-case."}
    capitalize {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent capitalize."}
    split {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent split."}
    split-lines {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent split-lines."}
    replace {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent replace."}
    replace-first {:reason "see :modules :kotoba.string :absent replace-first."}
    join {:reason "provided, but in kotoba.string.join rather than here -- see that module and :does-not-carry-join above. This is not a refusal to provide it; it is a refusal to make 74 modules pay for it."}}}

  ;; An ALIAS over kotoba.string: same surface, one implementation.
  ;;
  ;; Root ADR-2609040930's replacement-router table sends clojure.string to
  ;; `kotoba.text`, and that name resolved to NOTHING in this plane -- the
  ;; namespace in kotoba-lang/text is `kotoba.lang.text`, and no guest module
  ;; answered to kotoba.text at all (measured 2026-09-08). This makes the name
  ;; the router already points at resolve. The owner's decision the same day is
  ;; that kotoba.text and kotoba.string are ALIASES rather than a choice
  ;; between two names; the .cljc half of that is being done in kotoba-lang/text.
  :kotoba.text
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/text.kotoba"
   :kind :alias
   :aliases kotoba.string
   :provides #{byte-index-of utf16-index-of utf16-last-index-of
               starts-with? ends-with? includes?
               blank? trim triml trimr reverse
               index-of last-index-of}
   :equivalence :exact
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-08"
    :surface "identical to kotoba.string's, asserted rather than described: the oracle test compares the two public-name sets for EQUALITY, so a name added to one and not the other is red."
    :shape "forwarding wrappers, for the same measured reason as the clojure.string shim -- a bare re-export is :kotoba.error/export-names-undefined."
    :executed "(ns probe-text (:require [kotoba.text :as t]) ...) compiled to wasm32 AND web at amu 9092ee34 (exit 0 both) and RUN through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs and instantiateKotoba: (+ (t/byte-index-of \"a\u3042b\" \"b\") (if (t/starts-with? \"abc\" \"ab\") 1 0)) = 5 on both."}
   :hazards
   {:same-name-different-plane "there is also a kotoba.string in the .cljc plane, with a LARGER surface. This guest surface is deliberately a SUBSET of it, and the shared names answer the same thing on the same inputs -- held by the oracle, which calls clojure.string itself. A guest function that deliberately diverges from its cljc counterpart is named in :hazards on the canonical module rather than left to be discovered."}}

  ;; `join`, alone, because of what it costs the modules it would otherwise
  ;; share a file with.
  :kotoba.string.join
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/string/join.kotoba"
   :provides #{join}
   :replaces {join clojure.string/join}
   :equivalence :exact
   :why-its-own-namespace "join reads a [:list :string] with typed-list-nth, and a module containing that operation is refused WHOLE by an amu that does not lower it -- a caller that mentions only starts-with? cannot build. Keeping it here means only its own callers need the newer amu, instead of the 74 modules that require clojure.string."
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-08"
    :blast-radius "measured both directions with join still in clojure.string. Probe: (ns probe-str (:require [clojure.string :as s]) (:export [main])) (defn main [] :i64 (if (s/starts-with? \"abc\" \"ab\") 1 0)). At amu 9092ee34, --target wasm32 is exit 70 :error :wasm-typed-lowering \"unsupported typed Wasm expression\"; the same tree with join and join-from deleted and the name dropped from :export is exit 0. So it was join, and its blast radius was the module."
    :after "with join moved here, that same probe is exit 0 on wasm32 AND web at amu 9092ee34 -- the amu that could not build it before -- and both artifacts RUN, answering 1. The join probe itself is exit 70 at 9092ee34 (\"unsupported typed Wasm expression\" on wasm32, \"unsupported KIR node\" on web) and exit 0 on both at an amu carrying kotoba-script f803255e / kotoba-wasm 829966fd, where it RUNS: (join \",\" (typed-list-new [:list :string] \"a\" \"bb\" \"ccc\")) = \"a,bb,ccc\", with starts-with? still 1 in the same artifact."
    :removal-condition "when the consuming trees run an amu whose kotoba-script pin is at or past f803255ed259203e008d989f859b3b1b5fd22c98 and whose kotoba-wasm pin is at or past 829966fd47148d3ef2b873ea35bed57a63928606 (amu PR #898, merged to amu main as 8cc1361fa90ae084b4b5a3ef019caee9a1a097bb on 2026-09-08), this folds into kotoba.string and the namespace is retired. The function does not change; only which module can afford to hold it."
    :arity "the TWO-argument arity only: (join separator items). clojure.string/join also has (join coll), which concatenates with no separator; there is no multi-arity here, so that call is an arity refusal at check time. Asserted in the oracle test rather than described."
    :oracle "clojure.string/join itself, on the same separator and the same items: 16 cases covering the empty, single and many shapes, an empty separator, empty items, multi-byte separators and items, and an astral one. The test prints JOIN-CHECKED as failures*1000 + cases, so a run that checked nothing (0) is distinguishable from a clean run (16) and from one regression (1016)."}
   :costs {:join "one string-concat per item after the first, onto an accumulator rebuilt each step -- O(result length x item count) bytes copied. The list is bounded by construction (kotoba-sema max-list-items is 128) and the recursion is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion."}
   :hazards
   {:join-takes-a-list-not-a-seq "join's second argument is a [:list :string], not any sequence. Clojure's join takes any coll and calls str on each element; this takes strings only, and the element type is in the descriptor. What a caller can actually PASS is narrow today: a literal typed-list-new, or a typed-map-keys / typed-map-vals projection (:measured :list-construction)."}}
  :clojure.core
  {:path "lang/compat/clojure/core.kotoba"
   :provides #{ex-info ex-message ex-data}
   :equivalence :document-value-not-a-throwable
   :why "The abort ability carries a `:document` as its error type E, so the
         shape a .cljc module already writes -- a message and a data map,
         thrown, caught, read back -- moves across without changing at the call
         sites. What does NOT move across is the Throwable: clojure.core/ex-info
         returns an ExceptionInfo with a class, a stack trace and a cause, and
         this returns an ordinary two-entry document. It is not an approximation
         of a Throwable; it answers the three questions the trio is used for."
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-02"
    :document-error-type "(throw (ex-info \"empty input\" (document-map ...))) gives the throwing function the interface [:result :i64 :document] and the row #{:abort}; a bare `catch` binds the document and an explicit `(catch :document e ...)` pins it. NO frontend work was needed for this -- the E was already whatever the throw's operand types as. Measured against kotoba-sema f7c56ab3."
    :propagation "the document E survives slice-2 propagation: `read2` writes no throw and no try, calls `parse`, and the catcher two calls away reads the same message back"
    :oracle "clojure.core/ex-info itself, called from test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.cljk on the same message and data, asked the same two questions; the divergences below are asserted against it rather than described"
    :data-literal "(ex-info \"m\" {:input s}) does NOT compile -- a map literal is a constant document and `s` is a string local, refused with \"expression type mismatch: expected i64, got string\". The data argument is built with `document-map`."}
   :hazards
   {:no-nil "clojure.core answers nil for a missing message and for the data of a non-ExceptionInfo. There is no nil here: a document with no :message reads as \"\" and one with no :data as (document-null), which counts as empty. A caller testing for nil has to change the test."
    :non-map-traps "(ex-message e) and (ex-data e) TRAP with document-map-required on a document that is not a map, where clojure.core/ex-message ANSWERS for any Throwable. Nothing is silently wrong -- the program stops -- but it stops where Clojure would have returned a value."
    :arity "the one-argument (message only) and three-argument (message, data, cause) arities of ex-info are absent: a cause is another Throwable, and there is no Throwable here to be one. BOTH directions are now refused, and the message says the counts: (ex-info \"m\") gives \"function call arity mismatch: ex-info takes 2 arguments; got 1\" and (ex-info \"m\" data cause) gives \"... got 3\". Until 2026-09-02 the two were NOT symmetric -- the over-arity call was admitted and the third argument dropped without a word, which was a general hole in kotoba-sema rather than anything about this module ((two 1 2 3) on (defn- two [a b] (+ a b)) answered 3). kotoba-sema ADR 0011 closed it; the entry that pinned the hole, in test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.cljk, now pins its absence."
    :not-thrown-by-clojure "the document this builds is thrown by Kotoba's typed `throw`, which lowers to a result value. It never becomes a JVM exception, so nothing outside the guest can catch it as one."}
   :absent
   {ex-cause {:reason "a cause is a Throwable and there is none here."}
    Throwable->map {:reason "serialises a Throwable's class, message, data, cause chain and stack trace. Four of those five do not exist in this language."}}}

  ;; Not a Clojure namespace. The honest counterpart to one of the absent
  ;; names above, under a name that says which kind of index it answers.
  :kotoba.string
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/string.kotoba"
   :kind :canonical
   :provides #{byte-index-of utf16-index-of utf16-last-index-of
               starts-with? ends-with? includes?
               blank? trim triml trimr reverse
               index-of last-index-of}
   :moved-here
   {:as-of "2026-09-08"
    :names #{starts-with? ends-with? includes? blank? trim triml trimr reverse}
    :from clojure.string
    :why "owner decision 2026-09-08: kotoba.string is the canonical guest string library. clojure.string becomes a temporary shim over it (see that entry) and kotoba.text an alias over it."
    :what-changed "nothing about the functions. They are byte-for-byte the definitions that were in lang/compat/clojure/string.kotoba, held by the same oracle -- clojure.string itself, on the same inputs. What changed is which namespace owns them, and that one duplicate private helper (code-point-width, which both modules had identically) became one."
    :join-did-not-come "join is in kotoba.string.join. It reads a [:list :string] with typed-list-nth, and a module carrying that operation is refused WHOLE by an amu that does not lower it -- so putting it here would move the blast radius rather than remove it. Measured; see :modules :kotoba.string.join :measured."}
   :replaces {byte-index-of clojure.string/index-of
              utf16-index-of clojure.string/index-of
              utf16-last-index-of clojure.string/last-index-of}
   :equivalence {byte-index-of :byte-offset-not-character-index
                 utf16-index-of :clojure-index-with-minus-one-for-nil
                 utf16-last-index-of :clojure-index-with-minus-one-for-nil}
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-01"
    :oracle "a naive search over (.getBytes s \"UTF-8\") written independently of the Kotoba source, in test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_string_compat_test.cljk; 28 inputs, all agreeing, 18 of them with a multi-byte haystack"
    :divergence "(byte-index-of \"aあb\" \"b\") = 4 where (clojure.string/index-of \"aあb\" \"b\") = 2; asserted, not assumed, so the argument for the name cannot quietly evaporate"
    :route "amu check <entry> --source-path <app> --source-path lang/compat --jvm-free, exit 0, module-order [kotoba.string app]"
    :executed "not only lowered: compiled to wasm32 (2,279 bytes, exit 0, JVM-free), instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs, main() = 4n for (byte-index-of \"a\u3042b\" \"b\") and 3n for (byte-index-of \"\u3042a\" \"a\"). wasm32-browser also compiles (2,292 bytes)."
    :utf16 {:as-of "2026-09-02"
            :amu "bb51dc14"
            :oracle "clojure.string/index-of and last-index-of themselves, nil read as -1, on the byte-index-of input set plus the astral cases; asserted equal, and asserted DIFFERENT from byte-index-of where a multi-byte code point precedes the match"
            :executed "a three-module project (clojure.string, kotoba.string, app) compiled to wasm32 (4,426 bytes, exit 0, JVM-free) and instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs: (utf16-index-of \"a\ud83d\ude00b\" \"b\") = 3, (utf16-last-index-of \"abab\" \"ab\") = 2, (utf16-last-index-of \"a\ud83d\ude00\" \"\") = 3"}}
   :costs
   {:ends-with? "walks the haystack once by code point to find whether the suffix begins on a boundary; recursion here is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion"
    :trim "two walks (left edge, then right edge from there); triml and trimr one each; blank? one, stopping at the first non-whitespace code point"
    :reverse "one walk with one string-concat and one string-substring per code point"
    :utf16-index-of "the byte-index-of scan, carrying a UTF-16 unit count alongside the byte offset; utf16-last-index-of tries every boundary to the end and keeps the last match, so it never short-circuits"
    :byte-index-of "a naive scan: every code-point boundary of the haystack in turn, and at each one a code-point-by-code-point compare until the needle ends or a pair differs -- O(haystack code points x needle code points) steps worst case, and one string-code-point-at per compared code point. Recursion here is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion."}
   :hazards
   {:substring-prefix "(string-substring \"あ\" 0 1) refuses, so a substring-based starts-with? TRAPS where the answer is false. Compared by code point instead."
    :empty-needle-and-includes? "string-contains? refuses an empty needle (empty-string-search-needle) where clojure.string answers true, which is why includes? is not an alias. Named apart from :empty-needle below, which is byte-index-of's answer to the same question: the two hazards collided under one key when these functions moved here on 2026-09-08, and the EDN reader caught it -- Duplicate key: :empty-needle."
    :arity "each provided function is the one-string arity only. clojure.string's nil-accepting behaviour (blank? nil is true) has no counterpart because there is no nil string; a caller passing a second argument gets an arity refusal at check time, never a different answer."
    :cljc-plane-same-name "there is also a kotoba.string in the .cljc plane, with a LARGER surface. This guest surface is deliberately a SUBSET of it, and the shared names answer the same thing on the same inputs -- held by the oracle, which calls clojure.string itself rather than a table. Where a guest function deliberately diverges from its cljc counterpart, the divergence is named here."
    :no-contains-precheck "string-contains? would short-circuit an absent needle, but it answers the same question by a second mechanism; if the two ever disagreed this function would return -1 while a match existed. One slower mechanism beats two that must agree."
    :empty-needle "answers 0, matching clojure.string/index-of, and handled before the scan because string-contains? refuses an empty needle."
    :self-synchronising "the scan only looks at code-point boundaries, and that loses nothing: a valid UTF-8 needle begins with an ASCII byte or a lead byte, never a continuation byte, so a byte-level occurrence cannot start inside a code point."
    :minus-one-is-not-nil "utf16-index-of answers -1 where clojure.string/index-of answers nil. A caller who threads the result into arithmetic gets -1 where Clojure would have thrown; the name is the warning, and the option-valued spelling is waiting on :measured :option-return-on-wasm32."
