    {:reason "TWO reasons as of 2026-09-08, and neither is the one recorded on 2026-09-02. The accessor exists and lowers on both backends now (:measured :list-of-string), so a list CAN be read back. (1) CONSTRUCTION: a split returns as many segments as the input has, and a [:list T] can only be constructed with a statically known item count -- there is no conj, cons or append on one (:measured :list-construction). (2) THE PATTERN: clojure.string/split does not take a string separator at all. Measured 2026-09-08 on Clojure 1.12.4, (clojure.string/split \"a,b\" \",\") throws ClassCastException: class java.lang.String cannot be cast to class java.util.regex.Pattern. So a string-separator split is a DIFFERENT function from clojure.string/split, and by this module's own rule it does not get the Clojure name -- it would belong in kotoba.string under a name that says it splits on a literal, the same shape as byte-index-of."
