  ;; shape as byte-index-of above. clojure.string's three case functions call
  ;; String.toLowerCase() / toUpperCase() with no locale, so their answers
  ;; depend on Locale.getDefault(), which a guest cannot see; they stay absent
  ;; (see :absent lower-case / upper-case / capitalize) and these answer.
  :kotoba.string.case
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/string/case.kotoba"
   :provides #{simple-lower-case simple-upper-case upper-case-root simple-capitalize}
   :requires #{kotoba.string.case-tables}
   :replaces {simple-lower-case clojure.string/lower-case
              simple-upper-case clojure.string/upper-case
              upper-case-root clojure.string/upper-case
              simple-capitalize clojure.string/capitalize}
   :equivalence {simple-lower-case :unicode-simple-lowercase
                 simple-upper-case :unicode-simple-uppercase
                 upper-case-root :unicode-simple-uppercase-plus-specialcasing
                 simple-capitalize :first-code-point-uppercased-rest-simple-lowercased}
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-02"
    :amu "ca869d79e"
    :oracle "the Unicode SIMPLE mapping, in test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_string_case_test.cljk. Character.toLowerCase(int) / toUpperCase(int) and String.toUpperCase(ROOT) are the oracle where the host JDK's Unicode version is current; the Unicode 16.0 rows a Unicode-15 JDK answers as identity (U+019B, U+0264, U+1C89, U+1C8A and partners; Garay) overlay it. The sweep is every code point below U+3100 plus every one the JVM maps ANYWHERE (found by asking the JVM rather than by reading the table back) plus those overlay rows -- and, separately, 244 multi-code-point strings, because a claim about a string is not a claim about a character."
    :not-circular "the tables were GENERATED from these same JVM functions, so comparing the table to its source would prove nothing. The test runs the KOTOBA code -- block arithmetic, UTF-8 widths, string slicing, the walk -- and compares the ANSWER. Right characters at wrong offsets, a wrong block width, or a walk that lost a code point all fail it."
    :executed "compiled JVM-free to wasm32 through the project route (25,908 bytes, exit 0, amu ca869d79e) and instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs: simple-lower-case, upper-case-root and simple-capitalize all answer there."
    :divergences "asserted, not described: simple-lower-case \"\u0391\u03a3\" is \"\u03b1\u03c3\" where String.toLowerCase(ROOT) is \"\u03b1\u03c2\" (final sigma); simple-upper-case \"\u00df\" is \"\u00df\" where upper-case-root is \"SS\"; simple-capitalize \"\ud801\udc28bc\" is \"\ud801\udc00bc\" where clojure.string/capitalize is \"\ud801\udc28bc\"."}
   :costs
   {:walk "one recursive call and one table call per code point, and one string-concat per code point -- roughly two calls per code point, so a few hundred code points under the default 512-call budget. Above it the function traps on fuel rather than answering wrongly."
    :table "a linear cond over 60 (lower) / 66 (upper) ranges and 102 exact matches (upper-root). Those are instructions, not calls, so they cost no fuel; a code point past the end of the table walks all of them."}
   :hazards
   {:no-locale "these functions have no locale argument and never will. A caller who needs Turkish or Lithuanian casing is not served by this module, and must not read simple-lower-case as clojure.string/lower-case with the default locale -- it is Locale.ROOT's answer, always."
    :simple-is-a-unicode-term "SIMPLE case mapping is the Unicode name for the 1:1 mapping (UnicodeData.txt fields 12/13). Character.toLowerCase(int) implements it for the Unicode version the host JDK carries; on a Unicode-15 JDK that is not the published mapping for U+019B / U+0264 / U+1C89 / U+1C8A. It is not an abbreviation or an approximation; the full (String-level) mapping is upper-case-root for uppercase, and is absent for lowercase."
    :no-lower-case-root "there is no lower-case-root, because Final_Sigma is a condition on the surrounding string. Adding one means Cased and Case_Ignorable tables over the whole of Unicode."}}

